  "content": {
    "title": "Cross Season 3's Fate Revealed At Prime Video After Shocking Season 2 Ending",
    "text": "Published Mar 18, 2026, 12:14 PM EDT Jeff Dodge (he/him), a published author and graduate of Western Washington University, has been a TV news editor for many years and has had the chance to interview multiple reality show stars, including Randy Jackson, Nick Cannon, Heidi Klum, Mel B and John Cena. Fun Fact: he’s been to every single Idol Live! Tour. Sign in to your Screen Rant account Following the just-released season 2 finale, Prime Video has now revealed what the future holds for the crime thriller Cross. Prime Video announced that it has renewed Cross for season 3, which will consist of eight episodes, just like the first two installments. In a statement, Peter Friedlander, head of global television at Amazon MGM Studios, said that he's \"thrilled to continue this journey\" with creator and executive producer Ben Watkins as well as the \"incredible cast.\" He added that Cross has \"captivated audiences with its layered characters, pulse-pounding suspense and emotionally grounded storytelling.\" Cross, which is based on bestselling author James Patterson's Alex Cross series, centers on the titular detective as he solves complex cases and catches dangerous killers. The first season focused on the investigation of a murdered activist, while season 2 centered around a billionaire seeking protection from the FBI after a mysterious killer started targeting him. The finale, released on March 18, ended with Cross making the shocking decision to quit his job after the truth about Lance Durand was revealed, which will have major ramifications for the series going forward. Cross stars Aldis Hodge, Jeanine Mason, Wes Chatham, Isaiah Mustafa, Alona Tal, Juanita Jennings, Samantha Walkes, Caleb Elijah, Johnny Ray Gill, Melody Hurd and Matthew Lillard, with the latter actor, who has appeared in Scream and Scooby-Doo, joining the season 2 cast as Durand. Hodge is following in the footsteps of Morgan Freeman and Tyler Perry, who previously played the detective in the films Kiss the Girls, Along Came a Spider, and Alex Cross, which were released from 1997 to 2012. Patterson, who has written over 30 Alex Cross novels, and Hodge also serve as executive producers with Watkins, Craig Siebels, Sam Ernst, Aiyana White, Jim Dunn, Owen Shiflett, J. David Shanks, Patrick Santa, and Bill Robinson. Ian Watson/Prime When Cross first debuted in 2024, 40 million Prime Video subscribers tuned in during the show's first 20 days on the platform. By the end of 2025, Cross had the third-highest-viewed premiere ever for a Prime Video series. In 2025, Cross won Outstanding Drama Series and Outstanding Writing in a Drama Series at the NAACP Image Awards, with additional nominations for Hodge, Elijah, Hurd, Mustafa, and the stunt performers. Hodge has also earned nods at the Black Reel Awards for Television, NAMIC Vision Awards, and Gotham TV Awards. Cross seasons 1 and 2 are available to stream on Prime Video. Release Date November 14, 2024 Network Prime Video Directors Craig Siebels, Nzingha Stewart Cast Isaiah Mustafa John Sampson A skilled homicide detective balances his roles as a devoted father and relentless crime investigator with a penchant for hunting down murderers. Writers Ben Watkins Franchise(s) Alex Cross Main Genre Crime Creator(s) Ben Watkins Executive Producer(s) Ben Watkins, Bill Robinson, Craig Siebels, Dana Goldberg, David Ellison, James Patterson, Matt Thunell, Jim Dunn, Nzingha Stewart, Patrick Santa, Sam Ernst Producers Aldis Hodge Seasons 2 Streaming Service(s) Prime Video",
